E-1 / T2.8 -- does row alignment earn its memory?
=================================================

The BENEFIT side of X-1, which measured only the cost. Gates D-4, which was the
project's only PROVISIONAL decision until this closed it. NOT a
binCV-versus-OpenCV comparison: it compares binCV against binCV at four row
alignments, so ARCHITECTURE 10.3's denominator does not apply.

DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE MEASURING (TASKS.md T2.8, recorded in
EXPERIMENTS.md X-9 at commit 4245210, and in benchmark/alignment_benchmark.cpp):
  * speedup < 5% on all kernels     -> D-4 confirmed, close E-1, build no profile system
  * 5-20%                           -> D-4 stands; larger alignment documented as opt-in
  * > 20% on a per-frame kernel     -> reopen D-4, report before changing anything
"Speedup" means faster than the word-granularity baseline; a variant that is
SLOWER lands in the first band, and the size of the slowdown is reported rather
than clamped.

RE-MEASURED AT COMMIT 0072c1a. The numbers below supersede the first three runs
(commit 3383996). Two harness defects were fixed first and the fix was committed
before this re-run, exactly as the original benchmark was:

  * the physical-bound sanity check derived the cache tier from TRAFFIC (three
    images per bitwiseAnd call) and printed it as the working set, reporting
    "112.5 KiB -- L2-resident" for a batch that in fact holds 337.5 KiB inside a
    1.58 MiB interleaved round. EXPERIMENTS.md names that check as the validity
    control, so it is the one number that must not come from the wrong footprint.
  * measure::measureInterleaved restarted the body index at 0 each batch, so the
    input rotation degenerated whenever a single call already exceeded the time
    budget. It does not degenerate in THIS benchmark (batches here are thousands
    of calls), but the harness is shared with window_benchmark, where it did.

Neither defect touched a ratio: every conclusion below is the one the first three
runs reached, reproduced on a corrected harness.

PLATFORM: the reference device -- AUTHORITATIVE. E-1 is a cache question, which
is exactly what a laptop hides (EXPERIMENTS.md "Measurement platforms").

  device:           pi4
  cpu:              Raspberry Pi 4 Model B Rev 1.5 (Cortex-A72)
  arch / kernel:    aarch64 / 6.18.34+rpt-rpi-v8
  compiler:         g++ (Debian 14.2.0-19) 14.2.0, Release (-O2 -DNDEBUG)
  governor:         performance (restored to ondemand on exit)
  core pinning:     taskset -c 3
  throttled before / after: throttled=0x0 / throttled=0x0  (every run)
  benchmark commit: 0072c1a

THREE RUNS, because one cannot show that a ratio reproduced. Ratios are on
medians of 9 interleaved batches. TWO noise figures are reported, and the larger
one is what a difference has to clear:

  * batch spread -- (max-min)/median WITHIN one run, printed by the benchmark
  * run-to-run scatter -- (max-min) across the three runs, in the table below

They disagree, sometimes by two orders of magnitude, and only reporting the first
would have understated reproducibility. The starkest case is 94x60 / align 32 /
countNonZero: batch spread 0.1-0.3%, run-to-run scatter 11.4%. Read on the batch
spread alone, that row's 0.886x would have looked like a real 13% slowdown.

  640x480, uint32_t          run 1    run 2    run 3   scatter   footprint  AND fast path
  align 4 (word)  bitwiseAnd  1.000x   1.000x   1.000x       --     38400 B  contiguous
                  countNonZero 1.000x  1.000x   1.000x       --
  align 16        bitwiseAnd  1.003x   0.999x   1.015x     1.6%     38400 B  contiguous
                  countNonZero 0.999x  1.000x   1.000x     0.1%      (+0.0%)
  align 32        bitwiseAnd  0.305x   0.302x   0.309x     2.3%     46080 B  row loop
                  countNonZero 1.000x  0.998x   0.997x     0.3%     (+20.0%)
  align 64        bitwiseAnd  0.205x   0.207x   0.209x     1.9%     61440 B  row loop
                  countNonZero 0.996x  0.996x   0.995x     0.1%     (+60.0%)

  94x60 (pyr L3), uint32_t   run 1    run 2    run 3   scatter   footprint  AND fast path
  align 4 (word)  bitwiseAnd  1.000x   1.000x   1.000x       --       720 B  row loop
                  countNonZero 1.000x  1.000x   1.000x       --
  align 16        bitwiseAnd  0.996x   0.985x   1.000x     1.5%       960 B  row loop
                  countNonZero 0.990x  0.981x   1.000x     1.9%      (+33.3%)
  align 32        bitwiseAnd  0.978x   0.969x   0.969x     0.9%      1920 B  row loop
                  countNonZero 0.967x  0.886x   1.000x    11.4%     (+166.7%)
  align 64        bitwiseAnd  0.915x   0.878x   0.849x     6.6%      3840 B  row loop
                  countNonZero 0.944x   0.899x  0.897x     4.7%     (+433.3%)

READING. No alignment beats word granularity anywhere, on either kernel, in any
run. The largest number anywhere in the table is 1.015x, against a batch spread
of 8.6% on that row and a run-to-run scatter of 1.6% -- inside the noise on
either measure, and inside the rule's first band on both. So it is a null result
and not a small win. The rule's first band applies, unambiguously: D-4 is
confirmed, E-1 closes, and no profile system gets built.

WHERE THE SLOWDOWNS COME FROM, since three of them are large. Two mechanisms,
both consequences of choosing the alignment rather than confounds to subtract:

  1. ops/logic.hpp's contiguous fast path requires every stride to equal the
     words a row needs. Over-aligning disables it, and bitwiseAnd at 640x480
     drops to 0.31x (align 32) and 0.21x (align 64) -- 3.3x and 4.8x SLOWER for
     20% and 60% more memory. At 94x60 the width is not a whole number of words,
     so no variant takes that path and no variant shows the cliff.
  2. Padding words are allocated and never read, so a wider stride spends cache
     lines and bandwidth on nothing. That is the residual 0.85-1.00x at 94x60,
     and it is why the align-64 row is the worst one there too.

countNonZero, which has no fast path and walks rows unconditionally, is flat to
within 0.5% at 640x480 across all four alignments. That is the clean isolation of
the alignment effect on its own: on this core it is zero.

WHAT ROW ALIGNMENT ACTUALLY DELIVERS. binMat allocates with new[], whose
guarantee here is 16 bytes, so rowAlignment aligns the row STRIDE and not the
base pointer -- the benchmark prints the measured base alignment per run and it
varies (16/32/64 B) with the allocator rather than with the requested alignment.
So this experiment shows that making rows mutually congruent buys nothing; it
does not test absolutely-64-byte-aligned rows, which no binCV API can currently
request. Nothing in the MVP asks for one.

FOOTPRINT AND ABSOLUTE SPEED ARE NOT COMPARABLE WITH X-6's LOGIC NUMBERS, and
the difference is the fixture, not the kernel. This benchmark holds nine images
per variant (four input pairs plus a destination, 337.5 KiB at 640x480) and
interleaves four variants, so a batch streams from L2; logic_benchmark holds one
triple (115.2 KiB) and streams largely from L1. bitwiseAnd uint32 at 640x480
therefore reads 0.0327-0.0330 ns/px here and 0.0224-0.0235 ns/px there. X-6's own
working-set sweep predicts exactly that: it measured 0.02997 ns/px at a 96 KiB
working set and 0.03116 at 384 KiB. See results/logic_benchmark_pi4.log.

X-7 CAVEAT. binCV builds with no -march flags, so the countNonZero rows measure
fmov/cnt/uaddlv/fmov per word as much as they measure alignment. That lowering is
identical across the four variants, so it cannot bias this comparison, but it
does mean the absolute ns/pixel figures move if a dispatch policy is ever chosen
(ROADMAP 2.3). No -march flag was added.

Full output of run 3 follows.

